# Dedicated SELinux domain for the Tarish app (dev.tarish.app).
#
# WHY (security review, finding #28). The app currently runs as `platform_app` — a class every
# platform-signed app with its own uid lands in — so ANY such app can drive ITarishService:
# enumerate peers, send files, and setPolicy to switch on auto-accept. Giving the app its own
# domain lets the service's `find`/`call` be scoped to ONLY this app (see tarishsharingd.te),
# closing that hole.
#
# PERMISSIVE FOR NOW — this is being derived, not shipped. A custom domain does not inherit
# platform_app's broad grants, so the full rule set is collected from real denials on a
# permissive build (gos-selinux --collect while exercising the app: BLE scan/advertise, Wi-Fi
# Direct, notifications, file access, the share sheet, binding the daemon) and folded in below.
# The domain stays `permissive` until that set is complete and verified, and it must NOT ship
# enforcing (and never in a release build, which forbids permissive domains) until then.
type tarish_app, domain;
app_domain(tarish_app)
permissive tarish_app;

# The whole point of the domain: reach the Tarish daemon's binder service, both ways
# (the daemon calls back through ITarishCallback).
allow tarish_app tarish_service:service_manager find;
binder_call(tarish_app, tarishsharingd)
binder_call(tarishsharingd, tarish_app)

# --- derived from AVCs on the permissive build (added as collected) ---
# (nothing yet — first cycle establishes the domain assignment, then collect)
